<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahsevan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Şahsevən, Elsevan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shahsevan are Turkic groups of northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, living mainly in the districts of Mughan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, Kharaqan and Khamsa; their name means 'those who love the shah'. Shia Muslims who speak Azerbaijani, they were pastoral nomads who wintered in Mughan and summered on the Sabalan range. A story told by John Malcolm made them a tribe created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> to curb the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a> and Richard Tapper doubted it, and conclusive evidence of a Shahsevan tribe or confederacy dates only from the 18th century. Their chiefs opposed the Constitutional Revolution in 1909, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>'s army disarmed them in 1922–1923, and in 1986 they numbered about 6,000 families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahsevan.jpg" alt="Shahsevan" width="330" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Севрюгин Антон Васильевич · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shahsevan_girls_from_a_rich_family.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Where the Shahsevan came from is still unknown, and the ancestors of several tribes were of other origins, such as Kurdish, though Turkic identity and culture predominate. Between the 16th and the 18th century several tribal confederacies merged to form them. In the Safavid chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-beg-munshi">Iskandar Beg Munshi</a>, the expression shāhī-sēvan was used for an appeal to the faithful to rally round the shah, as Shah Mohammad Khodabandeh did during the uprisings of 1581 and 1584. Accounts recorded by 19th-century Russian officials made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> their homeland and traced the chiefs to a leader called Yunsur Pasha, stories the chiefs probably told to justify their rule and their pastures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1720s, with the swift collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> and Ottoman and Russian incursions, Mughan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> became a main field of conflict. In 1732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> restored Iranian control, deported several tribes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and seems to have united those that remained under his general Badr Khan Shahsevan. The Russo-Iranian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828 were fought over their land and cost them most of their winter quarters. In late 1909 most chiefs joined a tribal alliance against the Constitution and pillaged Ardabil, but nationalist forces under Yeprem Khan soon defeated them.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> of 1978–1979 some former chieftains were killed and others fled abroad. The new regime renamed the tribes Elsevan, those who love the people, but the name was not widely taken up and had fallen out of use by 1992. The anthropologist Richard Tapper judged that by the end of the century their pastoral nomadism did not seem likely to survive much longer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The tribes are arranged in descending units of tribe, clan, gubak, ube and family. The gubak, the basic unit, usually holds 20 to 30 families who drive a common herd between pastures. Ilbeks keep order and collect taxes, and the aksakals, the white-bearded elders, form the elite. The Shahsevan bred sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and horses, live in the felt dwelling called the alachig, and from the end of the 19th century began to turn to settled farming. Women weave kilims, jajims and horse blankets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shahsevan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A tribesman who forsook his tribe for the shah alone could join the <b>Shahsevans</b>, the “Friends of the King”, 12,000 strong under Abbas.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
